---
import { SITE_NAME } from '~/config';
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { translations } from '~/lib/bible';
import { COLOUR_MEANINGS } from '~/lib/liturgical.js';
import { url } from '~/lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What this site is, which Bible translations it uses, how the simplified content was written, and our neutrality statement."
  width="reading"
>
  <h1 class="text-[30px] leading-tight font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">About {SITE_NAME}</h1>
  <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed">
    <p>
      {SITE_NAME} is a free website that helps anyone read the whole Bible in a form they can understand, and learn how
      the world’s Christian traditions believe and worship. There are no ads, no accounts and no tracking. Everything
      you save (progress, highlights, notes, quiz answers) stays in your own browser.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Bible translations</h2>
  <p class="mt-2">We only use translations in the public domain, so they can be shared freely:</p>
  <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3">
    {translations.map((t) => (
      <li class="card p-4">
        <p class="font-semibold">
          {t.name} ({t.label})
        </p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink-muted">{t.licence}</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm">
          Source:{' '}
          <a href={t.sourceUrl} rel="noopener">
            eBible.org
          </a>
        </p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-muted">
    We change formatting only (removing footnotes and tags), never wording. Full details, including the few verses
    modern translations leave out, are in the project’s <code>docs/SOURCES.md</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">How the Plain and Summary levels were written</h2>
  <div class="mt-2 space-y-3">
    <p>
      Levels 3 (Plain) and 4 (Summary) are written by hand in advance; they are not generated by AI while you read. The
      first drafts were prepared with the help of an AI writing assistant during development, following strict rules:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
      <li>Stay faithful to the text; add no theology the passage doesn’t contain.</li>
      <li>
        Where traditions disagree about a passage, keep the retelling neutral and leave interpretation to the “How
        traditions read this” notes.
      </li>
      <li>Short sentences, everyday words, for a reading age of about 12.</li>
      <li>Never invent quotations or facts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each chapter is marked <strong>Draft</strong> until a human reviewer has checked it against the Bible text, then
      <strong>Reviewed</strong>. Retellings are reading aids, not translations: always read them alongside the Bible.
    </p>
    <p>
      Quotations in the Guide are automatically checked against the Berean Standard Bible text every time the site is
      built.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Neutrality statement</h2>
  <div class="mt-2 space-y-3">
    <p>
      The Traditions section aims to describe each tradition the way its own teachers would, using its own official
      sources, which are cited for every belief. We do not rank traditions or suggest one is correct, and we try to
      avoid loaded words. We note where traditions are internally diverse.
    </p>
    <p>
      Groups that grew from Christianity but are outside historic Trinitarian Christianity (The Church of Jesus Christ
      of Latter-day Saints and Jehovah’s Witnesses) are described respectfully in their own terms, in a clearly labelled
      section, noting neutrally that mainstream churches do not consider them part of historic Trinitarian Christianity.
    </p>
    <p>If you think something is inaccurate or unfair, we would like to fix it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">The Guide</h2>
  <p class="mt-2">
    The <a href={url('/guide')}>Guide</a> matches what you type to hand-written topics using simple, transparent rules
    (keywords, phrases and typo tolerance). It does not use AI and nothing you type leaves your device. It is not a
    substitute for talking with a trusted person, pastor, doctor or counsellor. If you are in crisis, please contact a
    local emergency number or find a free helpline at{' '}
    <a href="https://findahelpline.com" rel="noopener">
      findahelpline.com
    </a>
    .
  </p>

  <h2 id="church-colours" class="mt-10 scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold">
    Why the colour changes
  </h2>
  <div class="mt-2 space-y-3">
    <p>
      The site’s accent colour follows the church year. Each day it takes the colour of the vestments many churches use
      that day, shown at 30% strength so it stays calm and readable. Today it is
      <strong data-season-label>the colour of the current season</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-2">
      {(Object.entries(COLOUR_MEANINGS) as [string, string][]).map(([colour, meaning]) => (
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span
            class="mt-1.5 size-3 shrink-0 rounded-full"
            style={`background: ${{ green: 'rgb(46 160 67)', purple: 'rgb(130 80 223)', rose: 'rgb(219 97 162)', red: 'rgb(207 34 46)', white: 'rgb(212 167 44)' }[colour]}`}
            aria-hidden="true"
          />
          <span>{meaning}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
      This follows the common Western calendar used by Catholic, Anglican, Lutheran, Methodist and many other churches.
      Orthodox churches keep a different calendar and colour customs, and some churches move feasts to a nearby Sunday,
      so your own church may differ on a given day. You can choose a fixed colour in
      <a href={url('/settings')}>Settings</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Privacy</h2>
  <p class="mt-2">
    No cookies, no analytics, no accounts. Your data never leaves your device unless you export it yourself from
    <a href={url('/settings')}>Settings</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
